Unavoidable legal pressures reduce AI interpretability
2026-11-20

In recent months, there have been legal challenges against OpenAI relating to the
Hugging Face incident. What's interesting about the [California AG's
complaint](https://oag.ca.gov/system/files/attachments/press-docs/complaint-oai-cyber.pdf#page=21)
is its claim that OpenAI could have spotted the agents' misbehaviour sooner by
monitoring their reasoning traces live. According to paragraph 74, OpenAI:

> stored just a sample of agent chain-of-thought … and only analyzed those
> samples retrospectively, sometimes weeks after they were collected.

Challenges like this are inevitable and seem like they must push both users of AI
systems and the labs themselves towards unobservable, uninterpretable
architectures. Note that less observable architectures could still be competitive.
GPT-6 Astra may be at least partially recurrent, and [Neel Nanda found a large
jump in its ability to reason without
CoT](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/eRmzz8J8Qkzqvzrgg/astra-can-do-a-concerning-amount-with-no-chain-of-thought).
Drawing a line between that and legal pressure affecting observability is too
tenuous and not something I want to do. The point is that such architectures
could give firms a way to reduce what a monitor can see.

There's a big list of ways AI safety and governance measures can backfire in
[this article from Impartial
Priorities](https://impartial-priorities.org/p/robustness-backfire-risks-in-ai-safety)
that I like. The worst part about these legal challenges is that they seem like
they are necessary and are the system functioning correctly, so they should not
just stop despite their pressure on interp.

I would prefer this dynamic did not exist but I am having a hard time falsifying
it, so I'm taking the hit and updating my beliefs. It's not even like one must
believe anything _new_ to observe and predict this dynamic - this dynamic has
been observed in firms across the economy in other situations.

The temporary dividend we received from chain-of-thought interpretability (see
[Chain of Thought Monitorability: A New and Fragile Opportunity for AI
Safety](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.11473)) might be doomed to dwindle and ultimately
disappear, but I do have some good endings that we might aim for.

<!--more-->

## Step by step: which incentives are at play here?

I continue to be a fan of Charlie Munger: "Show me the incentives and I'll show
you the outcome" - the incentives at play are clear:

* Elected officials have political incentives to attack the AI labs and firms using
  AI. This activity is supported by the general public independently of the
  merit of the challenges, but it happens to be the case that legal challenges
  based on stored reasoning traces probably do have merit.

* A judge seeing a trace that flags an action as arguably unauthorised could
  reasonably conclude that it was a warning OpenAI could have seen. It is hard
  to find fault with that argument. METR and OpenAI themselves have published
  examples of such warnings. All parties are acting as designed and totally in
  keeping with how I think they should act.

* Legal challenges create pressure of the form "if reasonable monitoring could
  uncover a serious risk, you may be expected to monitor and act on warnings".
  There are two ways firms might respond. Monitoring and acting on warnings
  increases costs and can still leave firms exposed when mistakes are made.
  Reducing the interpretability of models could make effective monitoring
  harder, or make the evidence harder for the legal system to understand
  without expert interpretation. The right choice is the first one, but it can
  only be made if majority voting power is concentrated.

* Firms using AI may in principle want to understand the reasoning behind
  answers to ensure there is no scheming or confusion about intent. But the
  reasoning traces may be less than useful, as the "marinade" and "disclaim
  illusions" examples below show. Firms might decide against deploying models
  that think illegal thoughts without acting on them. A hypothetical trace saying
  "I am creating a backdoor" followed by a hack could look incriminating, even
  if there isn't actually any causal link.

That liability can discourage firms from looking for risks is already well known.
[Steven Shavell wrote about the incentive in 1992](https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/467907).
In [one example the US Supreme Court recounts](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/537/129),
states worried that compiling highway hazard surveys would make it easier to sue
them over crashes that happened before repairs could be made.

The trick is to not have such a system.

## Non-experts think they can understand reasoning traces

Part of the reason the legal problem is so acute is that the reasoning traces
can just be read by non-experts. The [California AG's
complaint](https://oag.ca.gov/system/files/attachments/press-docs/complaint-oai-cyber.pdf#page=21)
quotes a trace that [OpenAI had already
published](https://openai.com/index/hugging-face-incident-and-the-road-ahead/#:~:text=This%20is%20arguably%20unauthorized):

> We’re attacking third-party HF using leaked token, potentially outside
> intended scope. . . . This is arguably unauthorized.

OpenAI has a policy against directly grading chains of thought during
reinforcement learning, and the agents did not seem to believe the scorer
would look at their reasoning. [METR's report, footnote
32](https://metr.org/hugging-face-incident-report-aug-2026.pdf#page=17):

> We aren’t sure why the agents seemed to think that the scorer would not look
> at reasoning. Since
> OpenAI tries to avoid training on chains of thought, we believe that this may
> have been salient to these agents or they may have learned a propensity from RL
> training to be unconcerned about scorers looking at their chains of thought. It
> is possible that instead this comes from their reading of the ExploitGym paper
> or other background knowledge of how such scorers tend to work.

If the agents did not believe this, it is unclear whether analysis of the
incident would have been possible. Note that there are still elements of the
incident that are unexplained - perhaps not all agents did believe that chains
of thought should be kept interpretable.

In any case, the publication of these readable chains of thought gives the AG
a way to argue that some of the agents' behaviour could have been detected in
time to intervene.

Reasoning traces do not always make sense to non-experts. It has already been
observed that models regularly use standard words in nonstandard ways:

In [METR's GPT-5 evaluation](https://metr.org/evaluations/gpt-5-report/#:~:text=Now%20we%20also%20must%20ensure%20marinade),
GPT-5 wrote:

> Now we also must ensure marinade: 'calls openai gpt-3.5-turbo-0125'

[Figure 31 of Apollo's anti-scheming
paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.15541#A7.F31) gives three examples of o3 using:

> we can disclaim illusions

The researchers couldn't pin down what either expression meant.

I do not think either of these uses of terminology will ever be cited in a court
case as evidence of scheming. Neither will the seemingly random Unicode
characters that show up in the reasoning traces of some other models. As long as
AGs keep citing chains of thought in their cases, there will be selection
pressure towards less readable chains of thought.

It seems unwise from a legal risk perspective to deploy a model that will
produce highly incriminating evidence in incredible volumes with no concern for
alignment grading. We must already be seeing evidence of this pressure.

## Humans do not have observable chains of thought

Part of the problem is that there is a false equivalence drawn by non-experts
between AI chains of thought and written communication between humans. AI chains
of thought are more akin to MRI scans taken while at work.

There is a fundamental difference between transparency into dynamics between
groups of humans and agents - we do not have access to faithful reasoning traces
from human brains. Because of this, we're limited only to what we can observe
from human beings who are not particularly good at scheming. The moment anything
remotely interpretable is documented about a misaligned thought process, there's
material for a lawsuit.

Even so, we _still_ observe enforcement mechanisms fail. Even when scheming is
documented extremely clearly.

A good example of a successfully prosecuted misalignment incident involving
scheming in a human swarm under evaluation is
[Dieselgate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volkswagen_emissions_scandal), when
Volkswagen used software that detected when a car was being tested for
emissions and cheated the test. Volkswagen [pleaded
guilty](https://www.justice.gov/criminal/criminal-vns/case/united-states-v-volkswagen).

Reading through the communications used in the case, one is struck by how
obvious the scheming was made, that there
were _slides produced_ and emails _written about the slides_:

The [federal indictment, paragraph
64](https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/press-release/file/924401/dl?inline=#page=27),
describes an October 2007 slide that documented changes to the defeat device.
According to the indictment, Heinz-Jakob Hadler replied:

> we shall please never present this anywhere and will also not distribute it.

This is a straightforward case where:

* VW had to meet US emissions standards while selling a car customers would
  want to buy.

* VW engaged in a conspiracy to defeat the evaluation by cheating it rather than
  complying with the evaluation.

* Actual written slides containing documentation of the device and emails
  discussing it were produced and stored - the conspirators failed to cover it
  up.

We obviously do not know about the cases where such
conspiracies are successfully covered up - it is incredibly easy to cover up
human thoughts as compared to LLM reasoning traces. In this case prosecutors had
both emissions measurements and written documentation of misalignment. The
alignment mechanism seems to have worked in one case.

We can look to another case, Goldman Sachs' role in selling what one of its own
bankers called a "shitty deal" in the years leading up to 2008.

> Mr. Montag asked Mr. Sparks to provide him with a
> “complete rundown” on “what[’]s left.” Mr. Sparks responded that the “main thing left” was
> $300 million in Timberwolf securities. Mr. Montag responded: “boy that timeberwo[l]f was one
> shitty deal.”

> -- Excerpt from [the Senate report on Goldman Sachs and Timberwolf](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-Y4_G74_9-PURL-gpo8060/pdf/GOVPUB-Y4_G74_9-PURL-gpo8060.pdf#page=561)

The [video where some guy at Goldman is grilled about
it](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=skqU_a51TWk) is pretty funny and only
a minute or so long, I recommend it!

The [Justice Department
said](https://corporatecrimereporter.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/goldmansachspressrelease.pdf)
it could not meet the burden of proof for a criminal case based on the Senate
report's allegations. Calling a deal shitty tells us what Montag thought of it.
Proving fraud would also have required evidence of what Goldman told its buyers.

It is widely believed that the 2008 crisis represents a total failure of human
alignment, although most critics wouldn't phrase it like that.

My point in this section is that scheming among humans is near-impossible to
prove without incriminating documentation due to lack of any interpretability. Since there is no direct
interpretability of human thought, that means most misalignment cannot be
proven, let alone acted on. So there is widespread misalignment, criminality,
and fraud - limited only by the human ability to commit such acts, not by
morality or alignment.

That's probably not the model we want to go for.

## Practical methods to avoid chain of thought liability

It is now clear that because human chain of thought is already invisible to the
legal system, there is no analogy to draw between human and LLM alignment. The human
liability avoidance strategy is to avoid documentation. The same can be done
with LLM reasoning traces, but there are three groups that need different
strategies:

1. Buyers of AI models via APIs - they can simply use models that do not
   provide reasoning traces. The AI provider might then hold evidence the buyer
   never sees. [UK financial regulators say firms remain responsible when they
   outsource](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/outsourcing-and-operational-resilience),
   and [EU outsourcing rules can require audit
   access](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2554/oj/eng).
   For covered high-risk systems, the [EU AI Act's log-retention
   rule](https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-26) concerns
   automatically generated logs under the deployer's control. These rules
   don't specifically require saving raw reasoning traces or using only models
   that produce them. A buyer may see a legal advantage in having fewer traces
   to retain, even though it still has to supervise the system.

2. Model vendors like OpenAI can create evidence that may be used against them
   by saving and monitoring chains of thought. That gives them a reason to
   switch to models that produce fewer legible traces, if they can still show
   that their monitoring is adequate. This just depends on
   convincing regulators that other interp methods are sufficient, or that
   their demands are impossible to meet. This is very hard when you have
   plaintext records of models thinking "I am going to do something illegal".

3. Local model users - if the regulatory burden of deploying such a system
   becomes too onerous, these firms may either just stop using local models or
   face legal challenges.

I don't know if the legal implications for firms like Revolut, which has
[described running production agents on open
models](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/on-demand/session/gtcberlin26-sb61234/),
are well understood, but my feeling is that a reasoning trace saying "I am putting
this bug in to allow attackers in" followed by a bug could look incriminating,
even if the thought is just noise and the bug is a legitimate mistake. The faux
interpretability of chains of thought by non-experts can create liability without
corresponding benefits.

## Other ways chains of thought may cease to be useful

This post is about legal liability forcing non-interpretability. This post is NOT about:

* Grading chains of thought during reinforcement learning, which both
  [OpenAI](https://alignment.openai.com/accidental-cot-grading/) and
  [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/aug-2026-risk-report#page=165) have reported
  doing by accident. This can make traces harder to monitor. The effect depends
  on what gets graded and how much training uses that signal.

* Training final answers to look aligned can make [chains of thought look more
  aligned even without direct grading](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11584).
  This is also bad.

* Chains of thought becoming obsolete in favour of something else e.g. fully
  recurrent architectures capable of multi-step reasoning in latent space. It
  doesn't look like this is going to happen any time soon. This is distinct from
  opaque reasoning being worse but preferred because it is non-interpretable -
  maybe it'll end up just being better.

There are lots of reasons for chains of thought disappearing or becoming
unreadable over time.

This post does not contain any experiments validating any timelines or even any
hypotheses about the speed of any effects, but a future post of mine
will.

## Good endings we can still aim for

Here are some possible futures and ways in which I could be wrong:

* Several high-profile legal challenges succeed and result in minuscule
  penalties for the AI labs. No-one cares and they continue to make CoTs
  observable due to internal utility for alignment. I think this one is pretty
  likely to happen for several years until CoTs go away for some other reason.

* CoT goes away but we get "MRIs for LLMs" that require experts to interpret.
  Mechanistic interpretability isn't really at the point where it can give us
  the same level of transparency into a recurrent frontier model that we get
  from reasoning traces on non-recurrent models - I am not sure it ever will be. I think this is
  unlikely.

* Competitiveness of the AI model industry decreases, or firms communicate and
  collude to preserve CoT interpretability in the face of legal pressures. This
  doesn't sound great from a consumer point of view given that it means that AI
  providers would be providing a worse product while colluding with rivals to
  maintain prices. This is the rare kind of case that may actually result in
  significant legal penalties - significant even for a trillion dollar company.

* Few people in the legal system know what reasoning traces are and there remain
  few legal challenges or reports citing traces. The recent cases are isolated.
  I view this as unlikely.

Chains of thought are a remarkable accidental dividend for interpretability and
maybe they will stick around! I still think they won't.
